  "question": "Let $y = x^{x^{x}}$ for $x > 0$. Find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ using logarithmic differentiation, and further, express $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}$ in terms of $x$ and $y$.",
  "answeroption": "A",
  "explanation": "Take logarithm:<br /><br />$\\ln y = x^x \\ln x$<br /><br />Differentiate to obtain:<br /><br />$\\frac{dy}{dx} = y x^x\\left[\\frac{1}{x} + (\\ln x)(\\ln x + 1)\\right]$<br /><br />Let $F(x) = x^x\\left[\\frac{1}{x} + (\\ln x)(\\ln x + 1) \\right]$<br /><br />Then $\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = y\\left[ F(x)^2 + F'(x) \\right]$.",
